    "section": "backup-recovery",
    "html": "<p>Nitrobase uploads and downloads backups through a pinned <code>rclone</code> container (<code>rclone/rclone:1.71.1</code>), supporting both off-site object storage and local server folders. Storing backups off-site ensures you can recover even if a server is completely lost.</p>\n<h2>Supported Destination Drivers</h2>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n<table><thead><tr><th align=\"left\">Driver</th><th align=\"left\">Description</th><th align=\"left\">Key Configuration Fields</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td align=\"left\"><strong>Cloudflare R2</strong></td><td align=\"left\">S3-compatible object storage with zero egress fees (recommended).</td><td align=\"left\">Cloudflare Account ID (32 hex chars), Bucket, Prefix (optional), Access Key ID, Secret Access Key.</td></tr><tr><td align=\"left\"><strong>S3-compatible</strong></td><td align=\"left\">Amazon S3, MinIO, RustFS, Wasabi, DigitalOcean Spaces, and other S3 APIs.</td><td align=\"left\">Endpoint URL, Region (optional), Bucket, Prefix, Access Key ID, Secret Access Key, Path-style URLs (default on), Private/plain-HTTP endpoint toggle.</td></tr><tr><td align=\"left\"><strong>Backblaze B2</strong></td><td align=\"left\">Backblaze B2 native API.</td><td align=\"left\">Bucket, Prefix (optional), Application Key ID, Application Key.</td></tr><tr><td align=\"left\"><strong>Server folder (<code>local</code>)</strong></td><td align=\"left\">Folder on the app's server under <code>BACKUP_LOCAL_ROOT</code> (default <code>/var/backups/nitrobase</code>).</td><td align=\"left\">Optional subfolder path. Simple for quick local rollbacks, but lost if the server fails—pair with an off-site destination.</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>Adding &#x26; Testing a Destination</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Navigate to <strong>Data → Destinations → Add destination</strong> (<code>destination.manage</code> permission).</li>\n<li>Choose a driver and enter the credentials (all secret fields are encrypted at rest with AES-256-GCM Format v3).</li>\n<li>For S3-compatible endpoints, HTTPS on a public host is required by default (SSRF guard); enable <strong>Private or plain-HTTP endpoint</strong> only when targeting a private LAN/self-hosted S3 (such as MinIO or RustFS on your network).</li>\n<li>Click <strong>Test</strong> to verify the destination end-to-end: Nitrobase runs three checks—<strong>Write a test file</strong>, <strong>Read it back</strong>, and <strong>Delete it</strong>—and reports specific error categories (<code>auth</code>, <code>not_found</code>, <code>permission</code>, <code>network</code>, <code>tls</code>).</li>\n</ol>"
